---
/** How "Is it hot?" works, in plain words: a section of the About page on every site. */
import { BLEND, MODELS, VERDICT } from '@isithot/core';

const names = MODELS.map((m) => m.name);
const modelList = `${names.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${names.at(-1)}`;
const agreeExample = `"5 of 6 forecasts agree within ${BLEND.agreeWithinC}°"`;
---

<h2>Several forecasts, one answer</h2>
<p>
  Weather services run their own computer models, and they rarely agree to the degree. We ask
  {MODELS.length} of them for today's high: {modelList}. Some models do not cover every place, so we
  use whichever ones answer.
</p>
<p>
  With five or more forecasts we drop the single highest and lowest, then average the rest. That
  keeps one wild forecast from dragging the answer around. We do the same for the "feels like"
  temperature, which accounts for humidity and wind.
</p>

<h2>How much the forecasts agree</h2>
<p>
  Under the answer you will see something like {agreeExample}. Each dot on the strip is one
  forecast; the shaded band covers {BLEND.agreeWithinC}° either side of the blended high. When most
  dots sit inside the band, you can trust the answer. When they are scattered, the weather is
  genuinely uncertain, and it is worth checking a detailed forecast.
</p>

<h2>Hot for where you are</h2>
<p>
  24°C feels hot in Newcastle and mild in Seville. So we compare today's feels-like high with the
  long-term climate where you are: how warm your coldest and warmest months normally get. Half of
  the verdict comes from that local comparison and half from the temperature itself, because people
  feel both. A few common-sense limits apply: below {VERDICT.guards.notHotBelow}°C it is never
  "hot", and above {VERDICT.guards.notColdAbove}°C it is never "cold".
</p>
<p>
  The "warmer than usual" line compares today's high with the normal high for this date. Normals
  come from NASA's long-term records on a grid about 50 km across, so they describe the regional
  average; coasts, hills and city centres can run a degree or two either side.
</p>
